  "hints": [
    "Fix the right end. Which left ends give a sum ≤ K? Why do they form a contiguous range?",
    "Because all values are positive, moving the left end right only decreases the sum, so valid starts form a suffix `[l, r]`.",
    "Slide: add `a[r]`, while the sum exceeds `K` drop `a[l]`. Then add `r - l + 1` to the answer."
  ],
  "editorial": "For a fixed end `r`, positivity makes `sum(l..r)` strictly decreasing as `l` increases, so the valid starts are exactly `l(r) .. r` for some threshold `l(r)`. Moving `r` to `r+1` can only raise the minimal valid start, so `l` never moves backwards: two pointers apply. For each `r` the number of valid subarrays ending there is `r - l + 1` (zero if `a[r] > K`, when `l = r + 1`).\n\nTotal `O(n)`. The count can be about `n^2 / 2`, so use 64-bit counters; the running sum can reach `n · 10^9`. This approach **fails with negative numbers** because the sum is no longer monotone in `l`.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(1)"
  },
  "starter": {
    "python": "import sys\n\n# Count subarrays with sum at most K (positive values).\ndata = sys.stdin.read().split()\n# your code here\n",
    "javascript": "// Count subarrays with sum at most K (positive values).\n// readLine() returns the next line of input (or null).\n// your code here\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    // Count subarrays with sum at most K (positive values).\n    // your code here\n}\n",
    "java": "import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);\n        // Count subarrays with sum at most K (positive values).\n        // your code here\n    }\n}\n"
  },
  "solutions": {
    "python": "import sys\n\ndef main():\n    d = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n, K = int(d[0]), int(d[1])\n    a = list(map(int, d[2:2 + n]))\n    l = s = cnt = 0\n    for r in range(n):\n        s += a[r]\n        while s > K:             # positive values: shrinking always helps\n            s -= a[l]\n            l += 1\n        cnt += r - l + 1         # every start in [l, r] works for this end\n    print(cnt)\n\nmain()\n",
    "javascript": "const __L = [];\nlet __s;\nwhile ((__s = readLine()) !== null) __L.push(__s);\nconst T = __L.join(' ').split(/\\s+/).filter((x) => x.length).map(Number);\nconst n = T[0], K = T[1];\nlet l = 0, s = 0, cnt = 0;\nfor (let r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n  s += T[2 + r];\n  while (s > K) { s -= T[2 + l]; l++; }\n  cnt += r - l + 1;\n}\nconsole.log(String(cnt));\n",
    "java": "import java.util.*;\nimport java.io.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static StreamTokenizer st;\n\n    static long nl() throws IOException {\n        st.nextToken();\n        return (long) st.nval;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        st = new StreamTokenizer(new BufferedInputStream(System.in));\n        int n = (int) nl();\n        long K = nl();\n        long[] a = new long[n];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) a[i] = nl();\n        int l = 0;\n        long s = 0, cnt = 0;\n        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n            s += a[r];\n            while (s > K) { s -= a[l]; l++; }\n            cnt += r - l + 1;\n        }\n        System.out.println(cnt);\n    }\n}\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    long long K;\n    scanf(\"%d %lld\", &n, &K);\n    vector<long long> a(n);\n    for (auto &v : a) scanf(\"%lld\", &v);\n    int l = 0;\n    long long s = 0, cnt = 0;\n    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {\n        s += a[r];\n        while (s > K) { s -= a[l]; l++; }\n        cnt += r - l + 1;\n    }\n    printf(\"%lld\\n\", cnt);\n}\n"
  }
